        Welcome to the General Semantics Mailing List!

  I've set up the list in a very simple-minded way and I don't have
a lot of time for admin duties, so here are my current ground rules
and relevant info:

  o To send mail to the other participants, send it to the address:

        gs@lumina.ucsd.edu

  o For administration-type messages, you can either just send them
    to the above address (for now) or to me directly at:

        mcpherso@lumina.ucsd.edu

  o I've set up the list in a simple reflect/.forward scheme, so
    the list will not 'be' moderated or digested.  On the other
    hand, since I own the list, if a (future) list member for some
    reason becomes obnoxious (to be determined by my own inimitable
    semantic reactions :-), I may very well remove them from the list.

  o Regarding the "tone" or "atmosphere" of the list ... I like the way
    that Korzybski ran his seminars ... in a very "chummy", relaxed and
    open style (according to what I read in the 1937 hardcopy of that
    seminar), so I would encourage camaraderie and good fellowship.

  o Because of my simple-minded mail-forwarding scheme, you will have
    to use 'g' in elm (group reply) or 'f' in some other mailers
    (followup) to reply to list mail, else it will only go to the sender.
    Alternatively, just send all your messages to gs@lumina.ucsd.edu

  o If you know of others who have a keen interest in General
    Semantics, by all means have them contact me and I'll add them.

  o At some point I/we might begin to advertise in appropriate-seeming
    usenet groups (alt.self-improve, *.philosophy.* and *.lang*,.) and
    mailing lists (Extropians, novis,.)), so if you have any suggestions
    (or willingness to do it) let me/us know ... or just go ahead and
    do it ;-)

  o At some point I expect that new participants will be interested
    in obtaining a FAQ.  Would you like to put one together?  Also
    of interest might be a General Semantics ftp archive ... can you
    host it?

  o I won't require you to write in E-prime, although this list certainly
    can offer you the opportunity to practice it, if you 'are' so
    inclined. :-)

  o and, of course, "etc." ;-)

