Subject: [#94-8-92] Individualism vs Groupism (was FAQ 0.05)

Harvey Newstrom wrote 8/5/94: <Extropians value individualism more than
temporary group statistics....  Again, extropians value individualism and
diversity over conformity and noncreativity.>

  I frequently employ my memetic shock troops (polemical cannon fodder) against
group rights and for individual rights.  The two are utterly incompatible in any
practicable polity.  The encroachment of group rights into the power structure
of Western Civilization (it's always been the basis of other civilizations) has
proceeded in lock step with the degree of philosophical and theological
dogmatism and nihilism in the leadership elite.

  Group rights is a major part of Groupism.  Other identifiers of Groupism are:
credentialism (extremely debilitating to Pan-Critical Rational processes),
non-anchored self-identity (the only place of safety is in group affiliation), a
preference for ideological purity over truth, an infatuation with polling and
population demographics and a belief that "Equality" means making unequals
equal.  It is my impression that Individualism is of highest emphasis in the
Extropian Principles.  I think Harvey's point is relevant.

  There is truth in this passage from Ralph Waldo Emerson's "An American
Scholar" essay:

   "Is it not the chief disgrace in the world, not to be an unit;-not to be
   reckoned one character;-not to yield that peculiar fruit which each man was
   created to bear, but to be reckoned in the gross, in the hundred, or the
   thousand, of the party, the section, to which we belong; and our opinion
   predicted geographically, as the north, or the south?  Not so, brothers and
   friends..., ours shall not be so.  We will walk on our own feet; we will
   work with our own hands; we will speak our own minds."

  Groupism is disgraceful.

