As far as I understand it, I am forced to disagree with your
argument as it is too generally.  In order to have a society
of any sort, there must be some sort of limited "groupism."
Also, as a student of communitarian movements, I have been
surprised at the variety that I have found... some such groups,
to be sure, are highly ideological; I would not recommend them
to any liberaterian/extropian.  However, I have also found
some communes that exist because the individuals could not 
do what they want to by themselves.

Even Heinlein seems to have noticed this; a lot of the
people in his books live in large families that allow
division of labor, so that people can do what they
want.

The best example of "individualism run amok" was, as I
understand it, the French Revolution.  There, a person
was protected from ideological forces (to some degree)
by groups such as guilds and even (if one played one's
card right) segements of the Church.  After the revolution,
however, these mediating structures vanished...leaving
all people supposedly in control of the government,
but in reality bearing the brunt of a totalitarian
democracy.

As time I am driven to think that the formal structure
is not terribly important; rather, it is the spirit
of the individuals composing the group.

             John Bozeman



